From 5ce6e755782d80012411252f3e067e0bb54591ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: xuxh1 <18172956238@163.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 12:02:05 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(hydrology): finite lateral inflow to dynamic wetlands in
 place of the floor refill (candidate 17)

DEF_WETLAND_LATERAL_INFLOW (default off): a dynamic wetland tile no longer
has its water table held at the floor from an unbounded source. Every
step it takes the lateral inflow q_in = r R_up on the share that takes
lateral inflow (wetland_inflow_share, as C-38 and the area split), at any
water-table depth, as negative subsurface runoff through the same aquifer
exchange as the floor refill, net of the peat outflow, which now runs at
any depth (PEAT-CLSM Q = c T(zwt)). The exchange fills the pore space from
the water table up and hands the rest to the ponding store; the wetland
bucket spills ponding above wetwatmax as surface runoff in the same step,
so the column is never oversaturated. The floor refill and the area split
run only with the switch off.

Global runs (CoLMDRIVER, after the patch loop): R_up is the area mean
total runoff rnof of this step over the soil patches (patchtype 0, rice
paddies excepted) of the element, and r = min(A_up / A_wet,
DEF_WETLAND_INFLOW_RATIO_MAX) with A_up, A_wet the area shares of those
patches and of all wetland patches of the element (elm_patch). The value
r R_up is kept per wetland patch in the new restart field wetlatin
(written and read, with a default of 0, only when the switch is on) and
used at the next step, so the result does not depend on patch order. As
in ORCHIDEE-PEAT (Qiu et al. 2018; Largeron et al. 2018, eq. 1) the runoff
of the other soil tiles feeds the wetland; the cap, default 3, follows the
catchment-to-peatland ratios of 2.2 and 3.3 of two boreal peatlands
(Lambert et al. 2022) and the 2:1 to 3:1 upslope-to-fen ratio of Price et
al. (2010); prairie wetlands need 3 to 20 (Hayashi et al. 2016).

Single point (CoLMDRIVER, before the patch loop): R_up is the monthly
climatology DEF_WETLAND_INFLOW_SITE_RUNOFF(12) [mm/day] and r is
DEF_WETLAND_INFLOW_RATIO_SITE; both default to zero, a rain-fed tile.

The inflow taken each step is kept in the patch flux wetinflow [mm/s] and
written as f_wetinflow over the wetland patches when the switch and
DEF_hist_vars%wetinflow are on. The upland runoff is not taken off the
upland patches; with grid routing the wetland's negative runoff is netted
against it in the unit catchment, and the inflow of an element is bounded
by the upland runoff of the previous step.

Off, the hydrology, the restart files and all output are unchanged.

